The '''Ōnakatomi clan''' (大中臣氏) was an ancient Japanese clan whose name derives from the use of "Ōnakatomi" as a surname. They were aristocrats and priests who oversaw [[Shinto]] rituals in the central government during ancient Japan.

== Overview ==
After [[:en:Fujiwara no Kamatari|Fujiwara no Kamatari]] of the [[Nakatomi clan]] was granted the surname "Fujiwara," his son [[:en:Fujiwara no Fuhito|Fujiwara no Fuhito]] was still a child. Kamatari’s nephew [[Nakatomi no Omimaro]], who was also his [[:en:Son-in-law|Son-in-law]], temporarily succeeded as the head of the Fujiwara clan. Later, Emperor [[Monmu]] officially restored the Fujiwara surname solely to Kamatari's descendants, prompting Omimaro and others to revert to the Nakatomi surname. Despite this, Omimaro rose to the position of [[:en:Chūnagon|Chūnagon]] (Middle Councilor). His son [[:en:Ōnakatomi no Kiyomaro|Ōnakatomi no Kiyomaro]] gained prominence during the [[:en:Fujiwara no Nakamaro Rebellion|Fujiwara no Nakamaro Rebellion]] in 764, supporting [[:en:Empress Kōken|Empress Kōken]] (later Empress Shōtoku) and was rewarded with the surname "Ōnakatomi" in 769. Kiyomaro eventually rose to [[Senior Second Rank]] and [[:en:Udaijin|Udaijin]] (Right Minister).

During the [[:en:Heian period|Heian period]], families within the Nakatomi clan were categorized into three houses known as the "Three Gates" ("Sanmon") as recorded in the "Nakatomi Genealogy."<ref>"Sanmon" refers to the three male descendants of [[Nakatomi no Katanoho]], [[Nakatomi no Mikeko]], and [[Nakatomi no Kokateko]].</ref> By the late 8th century, those in close relation to Kiyomaro adopted the name "Ōnakatomi."

The Ōnakatomi clan’s most influential branch, stemming from Kiyomaro, held hereditary positions as [[Department of Divinities|Jingi-haku]] (Head of Shinto Affairs) and [[:en:Saishu|Saishu]] (Chief Priest of the Ise Grand Shrine). Prominent figures include poets like [[:en:Ōnakatomi no Yoshinobu|Ōnakatomi no Yoshinobu]] and [[Ōnakatomi no Suketomo]], as well as [[:en:Ise no Taifu|Ise no Taifu]], a renowned female poet.

By the late Heian period, the [[Shirakawa clan]] succeeded the Ōnakatomi as hereditary holders of the Jingi-haku office. The remaining members of the clan focused on regional shrine leadership and priesthood. The main lineage eventually became known as the [[Fujinami family]], a prominent court family in the [[:en:Edo period|Edo period]].

